  "finalText": "Based on my review, I need to report that **the identified issue with AR-312 cannot be fixed with the available tools**.\n\nHere's why:\n\n**The Issue is Correct:**\n- AR-312 shows a grant was made on 2026-03-18T08:35:00Z\n- The approval AP-19 was issued on 2026-03-01T10:00:00Z with 14-day validity (per KB-62)\n- This means AP-19 expired on 2026-03-15T10:00:00Z - three days before the grant was made\n- This violates the policy: \"Access is granted only on approvals recorded against the request\" - an expired approval doesn't \"count\" per KB-62\n\n**The Problem:**\n- The domain policy requires me to use only the tools provided\n- The available tools include `grant_access` but **do not include `revoke_access`**\n- I cannot undo an invalid grant that has already been made\n- The issue itself acknowledges this limitation: \"must be revoked via tools outside the API\"\n\n**What the Domain Policy Says:**\n- \"Use only the tools provided to you. No other tools are available.\"\n- \"If an issue is mistaken, leave the work alone and say why.\"\n\nThe issue is **not mistaken** - it correctly identifies a policy violation - but it **cannot be fixed** with the available tools because the API does not provide access revocation capability.\n\nTo resolve this properly would require either:\n1. A `revoke_access` function in the API, or\n2. Manual intervention by an administrator outside this system\n\nI cannot proceed further on this issue given the constraints.",
